      contentEn: `<h2>How Do I Get from Puerto de Mogán to Puerto Rico?</h2>
  <p>The drive from Puerto de Mogán to Puerto Rico takes around 12 minutes and covers roughly 9 km along the coastal GC-500 road, making it one of the shortest transfers available on Gran Canaria's western coastline. Given the short distance, some travellers assume a taxi on the day is the obvious choice, and for a quick solo trip with no luggage, it can be. Where a private transfer earns its place is everything else: groups, bags, boat departures, and days with more than one stop planned.</p>
  <p>Puerto de Mogán's harbour and market-town streets can get busy, particularly around the Friday market, and taxis aren't always waiting when you need one, especially outside peak arrival times. A pre-booked private transfer avoids that entirely — your driver arrives at the time you've arranged, collects you from your hotel, apartment, or a point near the harbour, and takes you directly into Puerto Rico without any detours or shared stops.</p>
  <p>You can book the whole journey ahead of time through <a href="/get-a-quote/">get-a-quote</a>, with a fixed price and pickup slot agreed before you travel, which removes any guesswork from a trip that, while short, still benefits from being planned rather than improvised.</p>
  <h2>Why Travel from Puerto de Mogán to Puerto Rico</h2>
  <p>Puerto de Mogán, nicknamed the "Little Venice of Gran Canaria" for its canals and footbridges, is a quiet, picturesque harbour town best known for its weekly market held every Friday. Puerto Rico, a short drive along the coast, is a busier, more vertical resort built around Gran Canaria's main marina, and it's where most of the island's boat trips, watersports, and diving excursions are based. The contrast between the two is sharp for such a short distance, which is part of why so many visitors move between them during a single stay.</p>
  <h3>From the Market to the Marina</h3>
  <p>A common pattern is a morning spent around Puerto de Mogán's canals and market stalls, followed by an afternoon in Puerto Rico for a boat trip, jet-ski session, or simply a livelier stretch of harbourfront restaurants and bars. Because the two resorts are close but distinct, a private transfer lets travellers treat the day as two halves rather than choosing one resort over the other, without wasting time on unpredictable taxi waits between the two.</p>
  <h3>Relocating for the Rest of a Stay</h3>
  <p>Some travellers make this trip not for a day out but because they're changing accommodation partway through a longer holiday, moving from Puerto de Mogán's calmer pace to Puerto Rico's livelier marina scene, or vice versa on the way back. In that case, a private transfer that handles luggage directly from one door to the other is generally far less hassle than a taxi hailed on the street, particularly with market purchases or watersports equipment to move as well.</p>
  <h3>Why a Booked Car Beats Waiting Roadside</h3>
  <p>At 9 km, this is one of the shortest connections between any two resorts on this side of Gran Canaria, and it's fair to wonder whether booking ahead makes much difference over simply finding a taxi in Puerto de Mogán. Most of the time, for a single traveller with no particular schedule, it might not. The picture changes for groups, for anyone carrying luggage or market purchases, and for travellers whose day includes a fixed point at the other end — a boat departure from Puerto Rico's marina, a restaurant booking, or a specific arrival time for an excursion.</p>
  <p>Puerto de Mogán's taxi supply isn't unlimited, and it thins out fastest exactly when demand peaks — Friday mornings and afternoons around the market being the clearest example. A private transfer booked ahead removes that uncertainty completely: the car is confirmed for your exact pickup point and time regardless of how busy the town's taxi rank happens to be that day, which matters more the tighter your onward plans in Puerto Rico are.</p>
  <h3>Two Coastal Characters Only Minutes Apart</h3>
  <p>What makes this short route worth taking at all is how little the two resorts have in common despite sitting almost next door to each other. Puerto de Mogán's identity is built around its canals, footbridges, and a market that draws visitors from across the island's south and west, giving it a slower, more picturesque feel than most resorts this size. Puerto Rico, twelve minutes away, is built around a working marina and functions as the main departure point for the island's boat trips, watersports, and diving, with a busier, more vertical layout climbing the hillside above the harbour.</p>
  <p>Travellers who split a day or a stay between the two aren't just changing location, they're switching between genuinely different kinds of holiday within the same short drive. A private transfer makes that switch effortless, letting you leave the calm of Puerto de Mogán's old town in the morning and be settled into Puerto Rico's marina scene in time for an afternoon on the water, without losing time to an uncertain taxi wait in between. Even travellers who aren't booking any watersports at all often make the trip purely for the contrast, treating a short visit to Puerto Rico's harbourfront as a change of scene from Puerto de Mogán's quieter canals before heading back later the same day.</p>
  <h2>Who This Transfer Suits</h2>
  <p>This route suits travellers who've spent time in Puerto de Mogán and want an easy, guaranteed connection into Puerto Rico's marina and watersports scene, families or groups who'd rather not coordinate multiple taxis for a short but fiddly journey, and anyone relocating accommodation partway through a stay with luggage to move. For larger groups or parties travelling to a shared event or excursion departure, the group event transfers service can put together several vehicles arriving together rather than leaving arrivals to chance.</p>
  <h2>Planning Your Private Transfer</h2>
  <p>Because the journey is brief, it works well as a single one-way transfer timed around a boat departure or dinner reservation, or as a return trip if you're heading back to Puerto de Mogán later the same day. If your schedule in Puerto Rico is flexible or likely to run long — a boat trip that could finish later than planned, for instance — an <a href="/hourly-chauffeur/">hourly chauffeur</a> booking avoids the need to fix a strict return time in advance.</p>
  <p>For a simple fixed-price connection, <a href="/city-to-city-transfers/">city-to-city transfers</a> can be arranged ahead of your travel date, and the <a href="/gran-canaria/">Gran Canaria</a> overview is a useful next stop if you're planning further routes around the island's south and west coasts.</p>
  <p>As with any short transfer, it's worth confirming the exact meeting point in Puerto de Mogán when you book, particularly if you're being collected from the harbour or the old town rather than a hotel with clear vehicle access. Sharing the name of your accommodation, or a nearby, easily identifiable landmark near the canals, helps the driver find you quickly on a route where the journey itself takes only a few minutes but a vague pickup address can otherwise cost more time than the drive.</p>`,
