      contentEn: `<p>Leaving Santa Catalina, a Royal Hideaway Hotel for a flight from Gran Canaria Airport (LPA) is a private transfer covering around 24 km and taking roughly 25 minutes, from the hotel's setting inside Parque Doramas in the Ciudad Jardín district down to the terminal on the island's east coast. Your driver collects you directly from the hotel entrance at the time you specify, works back from your flight to allow for check-in and security, and takes you straight to the correct terminal without any intermediate stops.</p>
  <p>Because the hotel stands inside a park rather than on an ordinary street, a driver who knows the exact access road into Parque Doramas avoids any delay at the start of the journey that could come from an unfamiliar taxi needing directions among Ciudad Jardín's quiet residential streets. From there, the route joins the GC-1 motorway heading south along the coast toward the airport, a road that runs largely uninterrupted between the capital and LPA, though the final approach can see a little more traffic when several flights depart close together around the middle of the day.</p>
  <p>Booking your departure transfer in advance through <a href="/get-a-quote/">get a quote</a> means confirming your pickup time, flight details, and exact hotel name ahead of your travel day, so there is nothing left to arrange once you are ready to check out. The same private service also covers the outward journey from the <a href="/airports/">airport</a> to the hotel, so both legs of your trip can be booked together if you prefer.</p>
  <h2>Leaving a Piece of Las Palmas History</h2>
  <p>Santa Catalina opened in 1890, making it the oldest hotel in Las Palmas de Gran Canaria and in the Canary Islands as a whole, a distinction that survives through a substantial 1951 rebuild by the architect Miguel Martín-Fernández de la Torre and the hotel's most recent renovation, after which it reopened in 2019 under Barceló's Royal Hideaway brand. Guests checking out after a stay here are leaving one of the few hotels on the island whose history genuinely predates modern tourism on Gran Canaria altogether, rather than one built specifically for it.</p>
  <h3>Checking Out from a Historic Address</h3>
  <p>Because Santa Catalina occupies a specific position within Parque Doramas rather than a standalone street-front building, it is worth confirming your exact departure point, whether the main entrance or a side access nearer your room, with hotel reception before your driver arrives. This matters more here than at a hotel with a single obvious frontage on a main road, since the park setting means there is more than one plausible point for a car to wait.</p>
  <h2>Timing a Departure from Ciudad Jardín</h2>
  <p>Ciudad Jardín's tree-lined streets and the surrounding Parque Doramas give this part of Las Palmas de Gran Canaria a noticeably calmer character than the busier parts of the capital, but that same layout means allowing your driver a little extra time to reach the hotel compared with a straightforward city-centre pickup, particularly if you are checking out during a period when several other transfers are also moving through the district. Once on the GC-1 heading south, the drive to the airport is a steady, largely coastal run with few complications along the way.</p>
  <p>Because the road connecting the capital to LPA can see brief bursts of heavier traffic around the middle of the day, timing your pickup with a comfortable margin before your flight is worth doing regardless of departure time, and your driver will typically build in extra allowance for check-in and security when working out your collection time from the hotel.</p>
  <h2>A Fitting Departure from a Historic Hotel</h2>
  <p>Guests leaving Santa Catalina after their stay are departing from a building whose 1890 origins, 1923 municipal purchase, 1951 rebuild under Miguel Martín-Fernández de la Torre, and 2019 reopening as a Royal Hideaway hotel together span more than a century of continuous use in more or less the same spot within Parque Doramas. Few departures from a Gran Canaria hotel carry quite the same sense of leaving somewhere with a documented, multi-generational history behind it, a detail some guests find is worth a last look back at the building's facade as the car pulls away from Parque Doramas toward the GC-1.</p>
  <h3>Fitting In a Final Morning in the Capital</h3>
  <p>Because Santa Catalina sits within easy reach of Las Palmas de Gran Canaria's wider landmarks, from Playa de Las Canteras to the historic Vegueta quarter, some guests use a final morning before an afternoon or evening flight to see a little more of the capital before checkout rather than spending it entirely within the hotel grounds. If this is part of your plan, it is worth confirming your pickup time with a little extra buffer built in, since a short excursion into the busier parts of the city adds a variable that a hotel-only departure does not have.</p>
  <p>Your driver can collect you from the hotel at whatever time you confirm when booking, regardless of how you choose to spend your final morning in Las Palmas de Gran Canaria, and flight tracking means any changes to your departure time are picked up automatically rather than needing to be relayed by phone. This flexibility is worth using if a stay at a hotel with Santa Catalina's history has left you wanting a little more time in the capital before heading to the airport, whether that means one more walk through Parque Doramas itself or a short trip further into the city.</p>
  <p>None of this changes the practical side of booking: pickup time, flight number, and any last-minute schedule changes are handled the same way as with any other departure transfer on the island, and the fixed price agreed when you book does not change based on how you spend your final hours in Las Palmas de Gran Canaria.</p>
  <h2>Booking Your Departure Transfer</h2>
  <p>If part of your stay in Las Palmas de Gran Canaria included day trips or excursions elsewhere on the island, our <a href="/hourly-chauffeur/">hourly chauffeur</a> service can be booked separately from this departure transfer, giving you a private car for sightseeing without tying it to your final airport run. When it comes time to leave, simply confirm your flight number and pickup time from Santa Catalina, and the departure transfer is arranged as its own booking, distinct from anything else you have used the car for during your trip.</p>
  <p>Flight delays or schedule changes after checkout are handled the same way regardless of how far in advance you booked, since telling us as soon as a change comes through is enough for your pickup time to be adjusted at no extra cost. Our <a href="/gran-canaria-airport-transfer/">Gran Canaria airport transfer</a> page has more detail on how the service works at the terminal itself if you would like to know what to expect before your car arrives. For guests travelling as a family or a larger group finishing their stay together, coordinating a single pickup time so everyone leaves Las Palmas de Gran Canaria in one trip rather than several is easy to arrange when you let us know the group size in advance.</p>`,
